---
title: "shared_object_sequencing (Sui Whitepaper: Shared Object Consensus)"
---
stateDiagram-v2
    direction TB
    [*] --> q_check_shared: transaction involves shared mutable objects
    q_check_shared --> q_owned_only: no shared objects (owned/readonly only)
    q_check_shared --> q_has_shared: transaction uses shared objects

    q_owned_only --> q_broadcast: use Byzantine consistent broadcast only
    q_broadcast --> q_cert_formed: certificate formed from quorum signatures
    q_cert_formed --> q_execute_owned: execute immediately (2 round trips)
    q_execute_owned --> [*]: return effects

    q_has_shared --> q_get_cert: form transaction certificate via consistent broadcast
    q_get_cert --> q_submit_consensus: insert certificate into high-throughput consensus
    q_submit_consensus --> q_observe_sequence: all authorities observe consistent sequence

    q_observe_sequence --> q_assign_version: assign version to shared objects per sequence order
    q_assign_version --> q_set_shared_lock: Lock_v[shared_ObjRef] = version from consensus

    q_set_shared_lock --> q_execute_shared: execute transaction with versioned shared objects
    q_execute_shared --> q_return_effects: Effects include shared object versions used
    q_return_effects --> [*]: return EffSign (4-8 round trips)

    note right of q_submit_consensus
        INV-001: Owned objects need only consistent broadcast (lower latency)
        INV-002: Shared objects require full Byzantine agreement (higher latency)
        INV-003: Consensus determines version of shared object, not sequential execution
        INV-004: Execution of different shared objects can be parallelized after sequencing
    end note
